<%= render "header", document: @document, heading: "Privacy Policy" %>

<h2 id="section-1">1. Who we are and what this policy covers</h2>
<p>Aixle Flow (the “<strong>Service</strong>”) is the hosted service at <a href="https://flow.aixle.com">https://flow.aixle.com</a>, provided by Dualboot Partners, LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company (“<strong>Provider</strong>,” “<strong>we</strong>,” or “<strong>us</strong>”), 5540 Centerview Dr, Ste 204, #24754, Raleigh, NC 27606. “Aixle” is a brand of Provider and does not refer to a separate legal entity.</p>
<p>This Privacy Policy describes how we collect, use, share, and protect personal information in connection with the Service and our pages at flow.aixle.com. It does not apply to copies of the open-source Aixle Flow code that others install and operate themselves: we receive no data from those installations.</p>
<p>The Service is offered only for business use. A company or other organization subscribes to it (the “<strong>Client</strong>”) under the <a href="<%= legal_terms_of_service_path %>">Aixle Standard Terms and Conditions</a> (the “<strong>Terms of Service</strong>”), and authorizes individuals to use it on its behalf (“<strong>Users</strong>”), each of whom accepts the <a href="<%= legal_terms_of_use_path %>">Terms of Use</a>. Capitalized terms used but not defined here have the meanings given in the Terms of Service.</p>

<h2 id="section-2">2. Our two roles</h2>
<p><strong>Client Data — we act for the Client.</strong> Everything submitted to the Service by or on behalf of a Client or its Users — workflows, prompts, tasks and comments, uploaded files, repository content, session records, and the Output the Service generates — is “Client Data” (Section 3.1 of the Terms of Service). We process it to provide the Service on the Client’s behalf and on its instructions. For personal information in Client Data, the Client decides why and how it is processed, and we act as the Client’s processor or service provider under the Data Processing Addendum (Exhibit B to the Terms of Service) and, where the parties have signed it, the Business Associate Agreement (Exhibit C). If your information is part of a Client’s Client Data — including as a User of the Client’s workspace — the Client is responsible for it, and Section 9 explains how to make a request.</p>
<p><strong>Accounts, security, billing, and System Data — we decide.</strong> We decide how to process the personal information we need to run accounts, authenticate Users, keep the Service secure, bill Clients, and measure and improve the Service. The Data Processing Addendum does not cover this information (Section 2.2 of the Data Processing Addendum); this policy does.</p>

<h2 id="section-3">3. Information we collect</h2>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.1 Account and sign-in information.</strong> Your email address, name, and profile picture URL; how you sign in (Google, Microsoft, your organization’s single sign-on, an emailed sign-in link, a password, a passkey, or an authenticator app) and the identifiers that method gives us; a hash of your password, your passkeys’ public keys, and your authenticator secret in encrypted form, where you use them; your memberships, roles, and invitations in Client workspaces; the answers you give during onboarding, such as your position and preferred language; and the times your account was created, invited, onboarded, and signed in. For each signed-in browser session we keep its IP address, browser user agent, and when it was last active, so that you and your administrators can see and end sessions.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.2 Records of acceptance.</strong> When you accept the Terms of Use, or the Terms of Service on behalf of a Client, we record which document and version you accepted, when, and the IP address and browser user agent you accepted from.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.3 Workspace configuration (Client Data).</strong> The Client’s organization name, email domain, branding, and settings; projects and who belongs to them; repositories; connected integrations; and the agents, skills, tools, workflows, and boards set up in the workspace.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.4 Credentials (Client Data).</strong> API keys, access tokens, and other credentials that let the Service reach AI services, integrations, and the Client’s systems, including the credentials of a User’s own account with an AI-assistant provider. We store them encrypted and use them only to provide the Service (Section 2.10 of the Terms of Service).</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.5 Content and session records (Client Data).</strong> The instructions and prompts given to agents; task text and comments; files Users upload; repository content retrieved into an agent session; files agents create or change; artifacts; a complete record of each agent session’s terminal; and, for agents where it is enabled, a log of the agent’s traffic to its AI provider, including the content of requests and responses. This content is stored as it was submitted or produced. We do not redact or filter it, so it can contain whatever Users or agents put into it, including source code, credentials, and information about people who are not Users.</p>
<p class="clause">The Terms of Service limit what a Client may submit. Unless agreed in a signed agreement that expressly covers it, a Client may not submit protected health information, payment card data, government identification numbers, financial account numbers, other sensitive personal information, or personal information of children under sixteen (Section 2.1(k)), nor personal data subject to the EU GDPR, the UK GDPR, or the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection (Section 2.6).</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.6 Information from connected systems (Client Data).</strong> When a Client connects a source-control, issue-tracking, messaging, or other system, we receive what that system sends us, such as commits, pull requests, issues, and the names and email addresses of the people who wrote them, and messages addressed to Aixle Flow in Slack or Microsoft Teams. Those people may not be Users.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.7 Usage and System Data.</strong> Data about how the Service is provisioned, used, and performs — such as the agents and models used, token counts, costs, durations, run outcomes, error rates, and the hours Workers are provisioned — which we use for billing, reporting, and running the Service. System Data does not include the content of Client Data (Section 4.3 of the Terms of Service).</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.8 Billing information.</strong> When a Client pays us directly, payment is handled by our payment processor, and we keep the customer and subscription identifiers, billing status, and invoices it returns. We do not receive or store full payment card numbers. When a Client subscribes through AWS Marketplace, Amazon Web Services shares the customer and subscription identifiers needed to set up the workspace and bill it, and we report usage to AWS for billing.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.9 Technical and diagnostic information.</strong> Server logs, IP addresses, browser and device information, and error reports. For a sample of browser sessions, and for sessions in which an error occurs, our error-monitoring provider records a replay of the web application in which all text and form input is masked and images and media are blocked.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.10 Communications.</strong> Messages you send to our support address, feedback you submit through the Service, and records of the emails we send you, such as sign-in links, invitations, and service notices.</p>
<p class="clause"><strong>3.11 Cookies.</strong> We use only the cookies the Service needs to work: a session cookie that keeps you signed in and protects your requests from forgery. We do not use advertising or cross-site tracking cookies, and we do not use third-party analytics.</p>

<h2 id="section-4">4. How we use information</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>To provide the Service</strong> to the Client, as it instructs and configures it, including running agents and workflows and connecting to the systems the Client authorizes.</li>
  <li><strong>To authenticate Users and secure accounts</strong>, including applying the sign-in rules a Client sets for its workspace.</li>
  <li><strong>To bill and meter</strong> subscriptions, including reporting usage to AWS Marketplace.</li>
  <li><strong>To communicate</strong> with Users and Clients: sign-in links, invitations, service and security notices, support, and notice of changes to our terms.</li>
  <li><strong>To protect the Service</strong>, its Users, and others: preventing abuse and fraud, investigating security incidents, and debugging.</li>
  <li><strong>To improve the Service</strong>, using System Data and aggregated or de-identified data, as Sections 4.1 and 4.3 of the Terms of Service permit. System Data we use to develop or train our products, services, or models does not include personal information and does not identify any Client or User.</li>
  <li><strong>To comply with law</strong>, enforce our agreements, and keep records of who accepted them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="section-5">5. Artificial intelligence and your data</h2>
<p>We do not use Client Data, inputs, or Output to train, fine-tune, develop, or improve any artificial intelligence or machine learning model (Section 4.1 of the Terms of Service).</p>
<p>Unless a Client’s Order Form says otherwise, the Service does not use AI models under our own account. Agents run on AI services the Client or its Users connect with their own API keys, subscriptions, or cloud accounts (“Client-Provided AI Services”). Content an agent sends to such a service is processed by that provider under the Client’s or User’s own agreement with it, and its settings — including retention and whether the provider may train on the content — are theirs to choose (Section 2.5 of the Terms of Service). Where an Order Form provides for AI services we procure under our own account, we configure them so that their providers may not train on Client Data or Output, to the extent the provider offers that setting.</p>

<h2 id="section-6">6. How we share information</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Within the Client’s workspace.</strong> The Client controls its workspace and may access and monitor Users’ use of the Service and what they submit to it. Other members of a workspace may be able to see the work done in it, such as tasks, workflow runs, and session records.</li>
  <li><strong>With our service providers.</strong> We use service providers — subprocessors — for hosting (Amazon Web Services), email delivery, payment processing, and error monitoring, under written agreements that protect the information. Section 7 of the Data Processing Addendum explains how Clients receive our current list of subprocessors and notice of changes to it.</li>
  <li><strong>With services the Client directs us to use.</strong> AI services, code repositories, messaging tools, and other systems a Client or its Users connect receive the content the Service sends them on the Client’s behalf. They are not our subprocessors, and they process that content under the Client’s own agreements with them.</li>
  <li><strong>With marketplaces.</strong> Where a Client subscribes through AWS Marketplace, we exchange subscription and usage information with Amazon Web Services.</li>
  <li><strong>For legal reasons.</strong> When the law requires it, or to protect the rights, property, or safety of our Clients, Users, us, or others. Where the information is Client Data, we tell the Client first unless the law forbids it.</li>
  <li><strong>In a business transfer.</strong> As part of a merger, acquisition, or sale of all or part of our business, subject to this policy.</li>
</ul>
<p>We do not sell personal information, and we do not share it for cross-context behavioral advertising.</p>

<h2 id="section-7">7. How long we keep information</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Client Data</strong> is kept while the Client’s subscription is in effect. For thirty days after it ends, the Client may ask to export its Client Data (Section 6.4 of the Terms of Service). We then delete it, or return it if the Client asks, except where the law requires us to keep it; copies in backups are deleted on our backup rotation, within ninety days (Section 10 of the Data Processing Addendum). A Client may delete content earlier from within the Service.</li>
  <li><strong>Account information</strong> is kept while your account is active, and deleted when it is deleted, apart from the records below.</li>
  <li><strong>Records of acceptance</strong> are kept as evidence of the agreement for as long as the agreement, and any claim under it, may matter. A Client’s acceptance of the Terms of Service is kept after the person who accepted it is deleted, without that person’s details.</li>
  <li><strong>Billing records</strong> are kept as long as tax and accounting law requires.</li>
  <li><strong>Technical and diagnostic information</strong> is kept for the limited periods our logging and error-monitoring systems retain it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="section-8">8. Security</h2>
<p>We maintain administrative, physical, and technical safeguards designed to protect the information we hold, including encryption of Client Data in transit and at rest, encryption of stored credentials, running each agent session in its own isolated container, and limiting our staff’s access to what they need to provide, secure, and support the Service. Our operational staff can reach account and session records, including complete session transcripts, through an internal administrative console when that is needed. If we become aware of unauthorized access to or acquisition of Client Data in our possession or control, we notify the Client without undue delay, and in any event within seventy-two hours (Section 3.7 of the Terms of Service). No method of transmission or storage is completely secure, and we cannot guarantee absolute security.</p>

<h2 id="section-9">9. Your choices and rights</h2>
<p><strong>Information in Client Data.</strong> If you use the Service as a User, or your information appears in a Client’s Client Data, the Client controls that information. Please send requests about it to the Client — usually your employer. If you contact us instead, we will let the Client know and help it respond, and we will not answer the request directly unless the law requires us to (Section 8 of the Data Processing Addendum).</p>
<p><strong>Information we control.</strong> Depending on where you live, you may have the right to know what personal information we hold about you, to access, correct, or delete it, to receive a copy of it, to object to or restrict how we use it, and to withdraw any consent you gave. To exercise these rights, contact us at the address in Section 13. We will verify your request before acting on it, and we will not treat you differently for exercising your rights. You may also complain to your local data protection authority.</p>
<p><strong>Your account.</strong> Your access to a Client’s workspace is managed by that Client’s administrators. To delete your account, ask an administrator of your workspace or contact us.</p>

<h2 id="section-10">10. Where we process information</h2>
<p>We are based in the United States and the Service is hosted in the United States. Our service providers may process information in other countries. The Service is not offered for personal data subject to the EU GDPR, the UK GDPR, or the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection unless the Client has signed a separate data processing agreement that covers it, including any transfer mechanism the law requires (Section 2.6 of the Terms of Service); where one is in place, it governs that data.</p>

<h2 id="section-11">11. Children</h2>
<p>The Service is for business use by people who are at least eighteen years old. It is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect personal information from them. If you believe a child has given us personal information, contact us and we will delete it.</p>

<h2 id="section-12">12. Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. The date at the top shows when it last changed. If we make a material change, we will tell Clients and Users through the Service or by email before it takes effect.</p>

<h2 id="section-13">13. Contact us</h2>
<p>Questions about this Privacy Policy, and requests about your personal information, can be sent to <a href="mailto:support@aixle.com">support@aixle.com</a> or to Dualboot Partners, LLC, 5540 Centerview Dr, Ste 204, #24754, Raleigh, NC 27606.</p>
